<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Low Countries</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Low Countries is the coastal region of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> comprising present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a>. Much of it lay within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> and later passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>. The revolt of the northern provinces against Spanish rule from 1568 created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a> and became a long drain on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/low-countries.jpg" alt="Low Countries" width="262" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NASA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NasaBenelux.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Low Countries form the lower basin of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheldt">Scheldt</a> delta. Regions without access to the sea tied themselves politically and economically to those with access, forming unions of ports and hinterland that reached as far as parts of the German Rhineland, so that hilly regions such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> are counted too. The name arose at the court of the Dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, who spoke of les pays de par deçà, the lands over here, as opposed to their lands in Burgundy. It later evolved into Pays-Bas. In the 12th century the region's trading centres rivalled northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> as one of the most densely populated parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> and councils governing most cities.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In Roman times the region belonged to the provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallia-belgica">Gallia Belgica</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germania-inferior">Germania Inferior</a>, and Frankish tribes entered it in the 4th and 5th centuries. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a> died, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-francia">Middle Francia</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-i">Lothair I</a> took in most of the region, apart from the County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>, which lay in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-francia">West Francia</a>. Afterwards the rulers of West and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-francia">East Francia</a> both coveted it, and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a> owed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> either to the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> or to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>.</p><p>In the 14th and 15th centuries separate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a> came under one family, the Valois Dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, and at the height of Burgundian influence the region became a political, cultural and economic centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-europe">Northern Europe</a>, famed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-netherlandish-painting">Early Netherlandish painting</a> in cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruges">Bruges</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghent">Ghent</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>. In 1477 the Burgundian holdings passed through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-of-burgundy">Mary of Burgundy</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>. Charles V, who inherited them in 1506, joined them as the Seventeen Provinces by the Pragmatic Sanction of 1549.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> policy of unification, with new bishoprics and laws against heresy, angered inhabitants who saw their provinces as distinct and helped to cause the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-revolt">Dutch Revolt</a>. After the northern provinces declared independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1581, the ten southern provinces stayed under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/army-of-flanders">Army of Flanders</a> and were called the Spanish Netherlands. They passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in 1713 under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-utrecht">Treaty of Utrecht</a>. The United Kingdom of the Netherlands united the region from 1815 to 1830, after which it divided into the Netherlands, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Low Countries consist today of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a>, the three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benelux">Benelux</a> countries, whose economic union grew from the London Customs Convention signed by their exiled governments in 1944. The term is sometimes extended to parts of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the German regions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-frisia">East Frisia</a>, Guelders and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kleve">Cleves</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Low Countries” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Treaty of Verdun (843 CE) formalised a threefold division into West Francia under Charles the Bald, Middle Francia (from the <b>Low Countries</b> through Burgundy to Italy) under Lothair, and East Francia under Louis the German.</blockquote><a href="/empire/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Its cloth industry made it, with northern Italy, one of the two great manufacturing centres of medieval Europe, and its towns pioneered forms of commercial life, from fairs and exchanges to urban self-government, that spread across the <b>Low Countries</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-flanders">County of Flanders · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The union of Hainaut with Holland and Zeeland from 1299 linked the French-speaking and Dutch-speaking <b>Low Countries</b> and prepared the ground for their later gathering under Burgundy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-hainaut">County of Hainaut · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1106 Emperor Henry V gave Count Godfrey I of Louvain the title of duke of Lower Lorraine, an old duchy that had once covered most of the <b>Low Countries</b> but had by then dissolved into a mass of counties and bishoprics.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brabant">Duchy of Brabant · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Salt from the marshes of Guérande and the Bay of Bourgneuf, linen and hemp canvas from inland weaving districts, and fish were exported, and from the fourteenth century Breton ships carried Bordeaux wine, salt and cloth to England, the <b>Low Countries</b> and Iberia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brittany">Duchy of Brittany · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Military power grew dramatically under the Valois dukes, who could draw on the wealth of Flanders and the <b>Low Countries</b> as well as the duchy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-burgundy">Duchy of Burgundy · Military</a></li></ul></section>
